# W1 — Swift `switchExplicitReturn`: swallowed arm `return` (value-arm vs statement-arm rendering)

Board row: `t-munu29i9-70b7` (branch `fix/swift-switch-explicit-return`, merge-back
`arch/agent-guided-governance`; claimed by `w1-fix-worker`, 2026-09-30).
Worktree: `dc-warn/w1-wt/wt/` (rsync of `dc-warn/swc-try-fix-wt/wt/`, which carries the
two verified boundary fixes SW04/SW05). Patch: `PATCH.diff` (single file,
`packages/compiler/reflaxe/swift/swiftcompiler/SwiftExpr.hx`). Raw logs: `evidence/`.

## 1. Verified diagnosis

The task's hypothesis is confirmed with one refinement. On the tree that actually runs
(SW04/SW05 already applied), the boundary wrap for an explicit-return arm **already
fires**: `armLines` computes `boundaryDestination = destination ?? currentReturnType`
(`SwiftExpr.hx:5675`), and a statement switch passes no destination, so the arm's return
value converts against the enclosing function's return type. The wrap is not the
residual bug. (On the original coordination tree — the archive's state — the wrap did
not fire either; that is the hypothesis as written.)

The residual mechanism, verified in code and in the generated output:

1. `stmtLines:823-824` dispatches a statement-position `TSwitch` to `switchStatement`
   (`SwiftExpr.hx:5520` pre-fix).
2. `switchStatement` reused the **value-mode** renderer: `switchReturn(sw, depth, true)`
   with no destination. `switchReturn` → `armLines` always close the arm by emitting
   `return <value>` — the value-mode contract, correct for return-position / expression /
   assignment switches.
3. `switchStatement` then **spliced the text**: for every line, `indexOf("return ")` and
   delete the 7-character prefix. For an arm whose last step is an explicit Haxe
   `return` statement (`variantArmPlan` classifies it as `OtherStatement(s, returnValue, …)`
   with `returnValue != null`), the `return` keyword is **semantically part of the arm** —
   it returns from the enclosing function. The splice dropped it, turning
   `case .one: return ReadOnlyArray(Gap.sourceFirst())` into the bare expression
   `ReadOnlyArray(Gap.sourceFirst())`.
4. Consequences, all observed in the baseline (`evidence/gap-fixture-baseline/`):
   - the arm value is an unused expression → 2 `swiftc` warnings at `Gap.swift:113:17`
     and `:115:17` ("result of 'ReadOnlyArray<Element>' initializer is unused");
   - control falls through every choice to the function's own trailing statement — the
     Haxe source's `return Gap.sourceFirst();` after the switch, faithfully rendered as
     `return ReadOnlyArray(Gap.sourceFirst())` — so **`.two` returned `sourceFirst()`**.
     Demonstrated at runtime: baseline binary printed `two-length=2`,
     `two-values=1,2` (`evidence/run-baseline.out`).

The Haxe source (`gap/Gap.hx`, `GapExplicitReturn`):

```haxe
public static function switchExplicitReturn(choice:GapChoice):ReadOnlyArray<Int> {
    switch (choice) {
        case One: return Gap.sourceFirst();
        case Two: return Gap.sourceSecond();
    }
    return Gap.sourceFirst();
}
```

The arms' `return`s are returns from the enclosing function; the trailing `return` is a
separate statement of the function body. The generated Swift collapsed the first into
discarded expressions and kept only the second.

## 2. The fix, and why it targets the cause

The cause is the **conflation of two rendering modes** (the "value-arm vs statement-arm"
class the board row names): `switchReturn`/`armLines` is a value-mode renderer, and
`switchStatement` force-converted its output with a textual splice that cannot tell an
explicit arm `return` apart from the value-mode prefix.

`PATCH.diff` (all in `SwiftExpr.hx`):

- `switchReturn` and `armLines` gain `statementArms:Bool = false` (default keeps every
  existing caller byte-for-byte on its old path).
- `armLines` tracks `valueIsReturn`: whether the arm's trailing value came from an
  explicit `return` statement (the value computation itself is unchanged — same
  `boundaryDestination` selection, same `arrayBoundaryText`/`expr` results).
- Final emission:
  - value mode (return position at `stmtLines:810`, `switchExpression`, `switchAssign`)
    and statement-mode arms whose last step is an explicit `return`:
    `return <value>` — the explicit return is **preserved** (it returns from the
    enclosing function, satisfying `currentReturnType`);
  - statement-mode arms whose last step is a plain statement: the bare value, no
    `return` prefixed and none stripped; the existing `nil` → `()` mapping is preserved.
- `switchStatement` becomes `return switchReturn(sw, depth, true, null, true);` — the
  fragile text splice is **removed**. (The splice also scanned every line for the
  substring `return `, so any line merely containing that text — e.g. a closure literal
  inside an arm declaration — would have been mangled; that fragility is gone.)

This is a fix at the cause, not the symptom: the trailing unconditional `return` was
**not** changed — it is the faithful rendering of the Haxe source's own trailing
statement, and changing it would have masked the defect. The arms now `return` their own
values, which is the value-returning switch the task requires.

Generated `GapExplicitReturn` after the fix (`evidence/gap-fixture-after/gen/gap/Gap.swift:110-118`):

```swift
public static func switchExplicitReturn(_ choice: GapChoice) -> ReadOnlyArray<Int32> {
    switch choice {
        case .one:
            return ReadOnlyArray(Gap.sourceFirst())
        case .two:
            return ReadOnlyArray(Gap.sourceSecond())
    }
    return ReadOnlyArray(Gap.sourceFirst())
}
```

The change is deliberately **not** folded into the destination-handoff repair (SW05):
different defect class, separate steps per the review ruling. `PATCH.diff` touches only
`SwiftExpr.hx`, and only the statement-arm path; `SwiftArrayBoundary.hx` / `SwiftDecl.hx`
carry the inherited SW04/SW05 state only (they are part of the worktree baseline, not of
this patch — the patch is the diff of `SwiftExpr.hx` between the pre-fix and post-fix
trees, and nothing else differs between the trees).

